[ ================================================================ ]
[ 070 -- locals and recursion                                      ]
[                                                                  ]
[     phoenix examples/070_locals_and_recursion.phx                ]
[ ================================================================ ]

def newline { 10 printa }

[ A `var` written inside a `def` belongs to the call, not to the    ]
[ program. Every call gets its own copy, which is what lets a       ]
[ function call itself.                                             ]

def fact {
	var n  n !
	2 n < if { 1 } else { 1 n - $fact  n * }
}

5 $fact print $newline

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     120                                                           ]
[                                                                   ]
[ Each nested call has its own `n`. If `n` were global, the inner   ]
[ call would overwrite it and the multiplication on the way back    ]
[ out would use the wrong number.                                   ]

[ !! A name is local only BELOW its own declaration. Above it, the  ]
[ same name still means the global one. This follows from `var`     ]
[ running where it is written, and it means one name can refer to   ]
[ two different things in one function.                             ]

var x  9 x !

def show_both {
	x print 32 printa      [ the global x, so 9 ]
	var x  1 x !
	x print                [ the local x, so 1 ]
}

$show_both $newline
x print $newline           [ the global was never touched, so 9 ]

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     9 1                                                           ]
[     9                                                             ]
[                                                                   ]
[ That is a trap, so declaring a local that hides a global is a     ]
[ warning -- this file prints one every time it is run.             ]

[ !! Using a name above its own `var` when there is no global to    ]
[ fall back on is an error, and the compiler says why:              ]
[                                                                   ]
[     def f { y print  var y  1 y ! }                               ]
[     error: undefined variable `y`                                 ]
[     = note: this function declares that name further down; a name ]
[             is local only below its own declaration               ]

[ !! Recursion needs a case that returns without recursing. Without ]
[ one it stops at 1024 live calls and says so, rather than eating   ]
[ memory until something else does.                                 ]

[ Local arrays work the same way -- see 080 for what `arr` is.      ]

def sum_to {
	arr work 4
	var top  top !
	var k  0 k !
	var total  0 total !
	dow {
		k 1 +  k @work !
		1 k + k !
		3 k >
	}
	0 k !
	dow {
		k @work total + total !
		1 k + k !
		3 k >
	}
	total top *
}

2 $sum_to print $newline

[ Expected output:                                                  ]
[                                                                   ]
[     20                                                            ]
[                                                                   ]
[ 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 is 10, doubled is 20. `work` is rebuilt on every    ]
[ call, so a recursive function may use one safely.                 ]
